WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
A DC fast charging stall can cost several times the price of the charger hardware alone, because

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.300
trenching, transformers, and interconnection work dominate the total.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:16.240
This is First Principles Daily, episode 64. A completed fast charging stall at a highway

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:22.100
rest area or retail parking lot can easily reach several hundred thousand dollars. The power

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:28.040
electronics, cables, and enclosure that actually move energy into a vehicle represent only a modest

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:34.040
share of that sum. Most of the expense sits in civil works, utility upgrades, and regulatory

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:39.740
steps rather than in the charger cabinet itself. This pattern points to an opportunity.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:45.880
Once the hardware floor is visible, the remaining cost becomes a set of solvable engineering and

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:52.060
process problems rather than an unavoidable fact of charging infrastructure. The contrast becomes

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:57.540
sharper when one considers that the same power electronics inside the cabinet have already

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:03.300
followed a steep descent in price over the past decade. Yet the total installed figure has

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:08.880
not kept pace because the surrounding site elements remain rooted in one-off construction practices.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:16.320
Current practice treats each stall as a custom construction project. Developers order a charger unit

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.840
from one supplier. Then they hire separate civil, electrical, and utility contractors to adapt the site.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:29.900
Trenching for conduit and cabling, concrete pads, drainage, and lighting are bid individually and

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:36.320
executed on site. Utility interconnection studies examine whether the local feeder can accept the

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:43.000
added load. When it cannot, the project pays for a new transformer or even upstream substation work.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:48.460
Permitting cycles run through city planning, fire departments, and environmental reviews.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:54.800
Each step adds calendar time during which capital sits idle. Demand charges from the utility

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:02.120
further raise operating costs once the stall is live. The instantaneous draw of multiple 150 or

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:09.900
350 kilowatt units can spike sharply even if average energy use stays moderate. Early market sites

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:16.200
often see low utilization, so the fixed costs of interconnection and site work are spread across

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:21.400
fewer charging sessions. Fragmented supply chains mean the charger manufacturer

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:28.860
optimizes only its box while the site contractor optimizes only its scope. No single party is responsible

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:35.000
for the integrated cost. As a result, the delivered price feels normal inside the industry,

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:40.560
even though the hardware itself has followed a steep cost curve downward over the past decade.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:47.060
One objection often raised is that every site presents unique soil, traffic, or zoning conditions

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:53.120
that prevent standardization. Yet this view overlooks how other repeatable infrastructure elements,

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:59.600
such as cell tower pads or modular substations have already moved toward prefabricated templates

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:05.300
despite similar site variability. Another factor is the financing structure itself.

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:11.420
Lenders price risk according to the longest lead time item in the stack, which is usually the

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:17.660
utility study rather than the charger procurement. Interests therefore accrues across the entire

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:23.760
project duration, even while the electronics sit in a warehouse. The net effect is that each added

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:29.080
month of delay multiplies the carrying cost without changing the physical content of the stall.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:34.680
A rough magic wand estimate begins with the major materials inside the charger

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:40.900
and the short run of conduit that connects it to the pad. Power semiconductors, copper bus bars

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:47.780
and cabling, aluminum or steel enclosure panels, cooling fans or heat sinks, and a modest length of

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:54.180
underground conduit together represent the physical content. Commodity prices for copper and aluminum

00:03:54.180 --> 00:04:00.100
fluctuate, yet their contribution to a single stall remains a small fraction of the installed price

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:06.680
once all site work is included. Adding the cost of a basic concrete pad and the simplest enclosure

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:12.660
still leaves the total material value well below the figures commonly reported for turnkey installations.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:19.400
Dividing the finished delivered cost by this material floor produces an idiot index that sits

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:26.200
in the tens rather than near one. That index shows design, process, and coordination steps account

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:31.640
for the majority of the price. The largest single contributors to the gap are the utility

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:38.180
interconnection study and any required transformer or feeder upgrades. Trenching and civil works that

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:43.840
must be performed in variable soil and traffic conditions come next. Permitting delays compound

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:49.940
carrying costs on the capital already committed to equipment and engineering. Because each of these

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:56.300
steps is executed as a one-off rather than as a repeatable module, learning does not accumulate

00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:02.600
across projects at the rate seen in factory production of the charger itself. The result is a

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:07.560
cost structure in which the charger hardware has already moved far down its own learning curve,

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:14.140
while the surrounding installation work has not. Consider that the copper and semiconductor content

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:20.100
might together equal a low single digit percentage of the turnkey figure. Once the transformer

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:26.420
upgrade, trenching labor and months of idle capital are layered on top, the ratio climbs rapidly.

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:32.700
An objection sometimes offered is that the utility infrastructure itself constitutes a durable

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:39.900
asset that benefits future stalls. Yet that asset is still sized and paid for on a per-project basis

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:45.820
rather than shared across a standardized fleet of installations. Soil conditions or traffic

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:52.220
management can double trenching time from one lot to the next. This variability is precisely what

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:57.800
prefabricated conduit stubs and standardized pad designs are meant to reduce by shifting the

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:03.320
variable work into a controlled factory setting where tolerances can be managed once rather than

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:10.040
rediscovered at every curb cut. A from scratch approach would first standardize the entire stall as a

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:16.180
prefabricated skid that arrives with conduit stubs, grounding and mounting points already integrated.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:20.440
That change would cut weeks of field trenching and concrete work.

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:25.680
Second, it would cite battery buffers at the pad so that the grid connection can be

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:32.020
sized to average rather than peak demand. That step would lower the transformer and feeder upgrades

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:38.500
required from the utility. Third, it would push for coordinated interconnection cues and pre-approved

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:43.320
site templates that utilities and planning departments accept without repeated studies.

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:49.800
Each of these moves shortens the time between order and revenue. Modular skids need volume

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:56.340
commitments from fleet operators or retailers. Batteries need continued declines in sell cost and

00:06:56.350 --> 00:07:02.610
clear rules for behind the meter storage. Streamlined permitting needs state-level legislation that

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:08.710
treats standardized chargers like other repeatable infrastructure. The genuinely hard constraints remain

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:14.670
local grid capacity that no single developer controls and demand charges that still penalize high

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:21.130
instantaneous loads even when average energy throughput is modest. Those limits will not disappear

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:26.850
through hardware redesign alone, yet they become more manageable once the variable site work costs

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:32.730
are compressed. Battery buffers introduce their own round trip losses and maintenance cycles.

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:38.410
When the alternative is paying for an oversized transformer that sits idle most of the day,

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:44.390
the net energy economics can still favor the buffer once sell prices reach the range already

00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:51.370
observed in stationary storage projects. Utilities have historically required custom studies to protect

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:57.550
feeder stability. Several regions have already begun accepting pre-certified skid designs when

00:07:57.550 --> 00:08:04.230
the developer provides certified load profiles and protection settings in advance. Scaling these templates

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:09.650
would require the first few projects to absorb extra engineering hours to prove repeatability.

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:16.310
After that, the marginal cost per additional stall falls because the studywork is amortized rather

00:08:16.310 --> 00:08:21.790
than repeated. When the charger cabinet is already inexpensive but the surrounding installation

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:27.710
still multiplies the total, the signal is that coordination and regulatory steps have not yet

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:34.070
been industrialized at the same pace as the electronics. Treating the entire stall as a repeatable

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:40.310
module rather than a construction project shifts the cost driver from field labor to factory repetition.

00:08:41.050 --> 00:08:46.850
The same pattern appears whenever a physical product reaches its material floor faster than the services

00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:53.930
wrapped around it. Tomorrow and every day after, one example or one opportunity. Which developer or

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:59.750
utility will first publish the line item cost of a skid based stall that bypasses conventional

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:06.370
trenching and interconnection cues. That's a wrap on today's first principles breakdown. The ideas

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:12.090
travel further when you pass them on. Send this to a fellow builder. I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:17.590
